WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.150 --> 00:00:03.150
Rockets are BIG.

00:00:04.380 --> 00:00:07.740
But no one seems to really know just how big these rockets are.

00:00:08.340 --> 00:00:10.580
And that's because any time we're shown these rockets,

00:00:10.590 --> 00:00:12.580
they're always just kind of like by themselves

00:00:12.580 --> 00:00:16.799
or flying through the air or next to a big building or even on a big boat.

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:19.050
You rarely get a good opportunity to get some visual

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:25.160
context for how friggin'  big these rockets are. For instance the SpaceX Falcon 9 rocket is 230 feet tall...

00:00:26.380 --> 00:00:28.000
But what does that mean?!

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:32.000
So today I'm going to show you what you'll never get to see in real life. Using visual effects

00:00:32.009 --> 00:00:34.259
I'm going to put these rockets in everyday places,

00:00:34.450 --> 00:00:39.809
so that we can all gain a better appreciation of the scale of these giant bottles that fly into space.

00:00:43.050 --> 00:00:48.170
To begin, let's take a look at the Falcon 9 landing legs here. And in pictures and videos

00:00:48.170 --> 00:00:52.279
they seem kind of dinky like maybe they're about this big or so whereas in reality

00:00:52.650 --> 00:00:57.049
they're 29 feet in length and when unfolded from the wall

00:00:57.060 --> 00:01:03.589
they will reach out 24 feet and to put that into perspective: our ceiling is only 17 feet tall.

00:01:04.190 --> 00:01:09.649
These legs literally do not fit in our studio and this begins to give you a sense of scale.

00:01:09.649 --> 00:01:15.720
So like I said, the Falcon 9 rocket is 230 feet tall meaning that it's about the height of a 21 story building,

00:01:15.740 --> 00:01:19.140
but the roof that I'm standing on right now is only seven stories tall.

00:01:19.140 --> 00:01:23.660
That puts me about a third of the way up the rocket and it just keeps going up.

00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:29.480
And this isn't even the biggest rocket that SpaceX makes. They have a more powerful one called the Falcon Heavy.

00:01:29.840 --> 00:01:34.980
Which is basically just three Falcon nines put together. So it's the same height, but way more powerful!

00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:40.080
So far they've only had a single test flight famously putting a Tesla Roadster out into the asteroid belt

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:44.400
to orbit the Sun for the next billion or so years and because these rockets are so tall

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:47.460
they appear to be kind of thin but as usual when you start getting closer

00:01:47.480 --> 00:01:50.920
to them their size becomes apparent, because they are 12 feet in diameter.

00:01:51.280 --> 00:01:56.060
I could literally stand on top of myself and still fit on the inside of one of these rockets.

00:01:56.320 --> 00:02:00.080
But here's the thing SpaceX is developing a much much larger rocket,

00:02:00.080 --> 00:02:02.240
because they need something that can go really far into space

00:02:02.360 --> 00:02:07.900
But also carry a lot of weight while they're doing it; like to Mars like a colony to Mars.

00:02:08.280 --> 00:02:11.040
So that's what they're making next: the BFR.

00:02:11.040 --> 00:02:14.960
Which stands for Big Falcon Rocket, because it's really Falcon big.

00:02:15.320 --> 00:02:21.120
At 348 feet tall the BFR is taller than the Statue of Liberty.

00:02:21.140 --> 00:02:28.120
Even if you were to lay the Falcon Heavy sideways on top of the Statue it still wouldn't be as tall as the BFR.

00:02:28.420 --> 00:02:32.200
This rocket is truly massive. With a 30 foot diameter

00:02:32.240 --> 00:02:37.540
it's as wide as a London bus is long. I mean just look at the fuel tank

00:02:37.550 --> 00:02:40.849
that's gonna sit in the upper spaceship part of the rocket. As of mid 2018

00:02:41.010 --> 00:02:46.459
this rocket doesn't technically exist yet except for the drawing board and in CG renderings like this. But this is it.

00:02:46.459 --> 00:02:47.690
This is what its gonna look like.

00:02:47.690 --> 00:02:50.960
In fact, I believe they want to get this up and flying within three years,

00:02:50.960 --> 00:02:56.240
maybe six years definitely. And as big as these Rockets are we may someday see rockets that are even larger.

00:02:56.240 --> 00:03:00.940
I mean the BFR is still not as large as the Saturn 5 rocket which took us to the moon.

00:03:00.940 --> 00:03:02.900
And we built that 50 years ago!

00:03:02.900 --> 00:03:06.400
In the end I just find it crazy that these structures are some of the

00:03:06.570 --> 00:03:11.329
fastest vehicles humanity has ever made, because going into space is not just simply a matter of going up.

00:03:11.329 --> 00:03:17.000
It's a matter of going up and then sideways so fast, that you just continue to fall around the earth.

00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:22.660
And those speeds are 17,000 miles an hour. At sea level that is Mach 22.

00:03:30.180 --> 00:03:33.260
Thanks for watching this video it was a pretty fun experiment to make and if you liked it

00:03:33.260 --> 00:03:37.180
I think I might try making more of these. In the meantime try sharing this video with your friends

00:03:37.180 --> 00:03:40.200
and asking them first how big that they think rockets are

00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:42.760
because I'm willing to bet they're gonna underestimate it.

